INT. HOLLOW OAK - THUNDERSTRUCK TRUNK - NIGHT

A cavern of living wood, blackened where lightning split the crown. Fireflies drift in the rafters. A ring of stumps serves as a council table. Rain drums on the bark overhead.

EIRA, a tall woman with silver braids and a staff of rowan, stands at the head of the ring. THORVALD, a broad-shouldered hunter in a bearskin, sits on the nearest stump with his axe across his knees. CALLA, a slight healer in a moss-green shawl, kneels at the center and lays herbs on a flat stone. BRENNUS, a gaunt scholar with ink-stained fingers, unrolls a map across his lap.

EIRA
We are gathered.

THORVALD
We are late.

EIRA
We are as late as the storm allows.

CALLA
(grinding herbs)
The river rose another foot at dusk.

BRENNUS
Two feet, by my measure.

THORVALD
Your measure is a stick.

BRENNUS
It is a calibrated stick.

Eira raps her staff on the floor. The fireflies scatter and re-gather.

EIRA
The question before us is the pass. Do we hold it, or do we abandon the valley?

THORVALD
We hold.

CALLA
We cannot hold. We have no healers to spare.

BRENNUS
We have three hundred archers and a wall.

THORVALD
We have six hundred when the Greycloaks arrive.

CALLA
If they arrive.

EIRA
They will arrive.

She walks slowly around the ring. Thorvald watches her with open suspicion. Brennus watches the map. Calla watches the stone.

EIRA (CONT'D)
Brennus. The pass, on the map.

He holds it up. She traces a line with the tip of her staff.

BRENNUS
Here. A single path, fifty paces wide.

EIRA
And the wall?

BRENNUS
Here. At the narrowest point.

THORVALD
Then we fight at the wall.

EIRA
We fight at the wall, and Brennus commands the archers.

THORVALD
He has never drawn a bow.

BRENNUS
I have read forty books about them.

THORVALD
That is not the same.

CALLA
Enough. He can count better than any of us.

Thorvald grunts and looks away.

EIRA
Then it is decided. Thorvald takes the left. Brennus the center. Calla tends the wounded in the hollow.

CALLA
And you?

EIRA
I will meet the Greycloaks on the road, and I will not be gentle.

The council falls silent. Rain drums. A single firefly settles on the staff, glowing steady and gold.

THORVALD
Eira. If this goes badly...

EIRA
It will not.

THORVALD
If it does.

EIRA
Then you will hold the wall until sunrise, and Calla will bury us in the roots.

CALLA
I will bury you in the sweet roots, by the spring.

EIRA
Thank you.

She raises the staff. One by one, they rise and place a hand upon it. The light of the fireflies swells around them.
